+### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
+*   **Primitive Explosion:** Setting $P_{fin}$ too large can cause primitive explosion and Out-Of-Memory (OOM) errors [S1]. However, the double-condition densification scheme prevents this by capping growth via top-$k$ selection [S1].
+*   **Removing Threshold:** Removing the threshold condition and keeping only top-$k$ selection forces $N_{GS}$ to exactly equal $P_{fin}$ after optimization, which may be undesirable if $P_{fin}$ is too large [S1].
+*   **Scheduler Removal:** Setting $P_{fin}$ to infinity (removing the scheduler) still yields stable $N_{GS}$ approximating the backbone's primitive count, indicating the growth is stable and safe [S1].
+*   **Hyper-parameter Sensitivity:** A larger $\gamma$ increases $P_{fin}$, leading to larger $N_{GS}$, higher quality, and longer optimization time [S1]. A larger $a$ reduces initial resolution, significantly reducing optimization time with little impact on quality [S1].
 
